<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<object type="traktor.script.Script" version="2">
	<text><![CDATA[#using \{6E9AD100-0000-4000-8000-000000000010}

import(traktor)

-- Weapon
--
-- Base class for anything the player carries and uses. It exists so that the Stage does
-- not have to know what is in the player's hands: Main routes buttons and frames at a
-- Scripts/Weapons/Loadout, the loadout routes them at whichever Weapon is drawn, and what
-- a trigger pull actually *does* lives in the subclass -- Scripts/Weapons/Rifle,
-- Scripts/Weapons/Grenades, Scripts/Weapons/Melee.
--
-- Adding a weapon is therefore a subclass and one line in the Loadout, with nothing to
-- change in Main. What is below is the whole of what the stage promises to call, and every
-- method has a default here, so a subclass writes only the ones it has an opinion about.
--
-- `context` is the Stage -- the Main instance -- exactly as in Scripts/Pickup, and it is
-- the only thing a weapon is handed. What it may ask of it (see "what a weapon is given"
-- in Main, which is where these are defined):
--
--   context:eye()                  where a shot, a swing or a throw starts from
--   context:aim()                  ...the rotation it goes out along, and
--   context:aimDirection()         the same thing as a direction
--   context:player()               the Humanoid entity, or nil before it is resolved
--   context:isRunning()            sprinting, so a view model can drop to a low ready
--   context:isCarrying()           a relic fills the off hand, so a view model moves over
--   context:killEnemy(e, p, i, w)  the one way anything in this game dies
--   context:isEnemyDead(e)         ...and whether it already has
--   context:shoveCorpse(...)       ...and what hitting one that already has does
--   context:knockbackImpulse(dir)  what a corpse is shoved with
--   context:reportGunshot(x, z)    published to the horde, which comes to look
--   context:kickCrosshair()        recoil bloom on the reticle
--   context:raisePlayerEffect(...) a puff of dust where a round landed
--   context.world                  the WorldLayer, for building and removing entities
--   context.environment            physics, input and resources
--
-- Everything a weapon needs about where the player is looking comes through those first
-- three rather than off the fields behind them, which is what keeps this from being a
-- second copy of the aiming code: change how the player aims and every weapon follows.
--
-- Cadence is the one piece of behaviour the base owns outright, because every weapon has
-- it and no two agree on the number: set _interval in the constructor, ask self:ready()
-- before acting, and set self._cooldown = self._interval when you do.

Weapon = Weapon or class("Weapon")

function Weapon:new(context)
	self._context = context
	self._interval = 0.0   -- minimum seconds between uses; the subclass sets it
	self._cooldown = 0.0   -- ...and counts down to the next one being allowed
end

-- What this weapon is called. The loadout looks weapons up by it (Loadout:find), and it
-- is the name a save file would remember a drawn weapon by, so keep it stable.
function Weapon:slot()
	return "weapon"
end

-- What a pickup has to be carrying for this weapon to want it -- "rounds", "grenades" --
-- or nil for a weapon that takes no ammunition at all.
--
-- This is what keeps the crates as ignorant of the arsenal as the stage is:
-- Scripts/AmmoPickup and Scripts/GrenadePickup name a kind, and the loadout hands it to
-- whichever weapon answers to that kind (Loadout:supply). A new weapon with a new kind of
-- ammunition needs a new crate and nothing else.
function Weapon:supplyKind()
	return nil
end

-- Live handles off the world: a view model entity, its event set, its trail. Called every
-- frame from Loadout:resolve, exactly as Main:resolve is called from Main:update -- so it
-- has to be cheap once it has what it wants, and it has to cope with a world that is not
-- there yet.
function Weapon:resolve()
end

-- Let go of every live entity handle, for a script hot reload: the world comes back new
-- and anything held here belongs to the old one. Plain numbers -- rounds carried,
-- grenades in the pouch -- deliberately stay, which is the point of the split: a reload
-- must not quietly resupply the player. See Main:reload.
function Weapon:reset()
end

-- Does this weapon show a tile on the HUD row? Asked before the row is laid out, because
-- a TableLayout is built with its column list and cannot grow one afterwards -- see
-- Loadout:hudColumns and the HUD block in Main:new.
function Weapon:hasHud()
	return false
end

-- Build that tile into the bottom row. Called once, from Main:new.
function Weapon:createHud(parent)
	return nil
end

-- Tick the tile's own animation. Called from Main:update *above* its freeze returns, so a
-- magazine spent on the last frame before a pause still finishes counting down.
function Weapon:updateHud(dt)
end

-- The drawn weapon changed. Only of interest to a tile that says whether the trigger is
-- currently on this weapon (see Grenades:_refreshHud).
function Weapon:selectionChanged(selected)
end

-- One simulation step of the weapon itself -- below Main's freeze returns, so a reload in
-- flight and a grenade in the air both stop with the rest of the game.
--
-- Every weapon in the loadout is ticked, not only the drawn one: a reload continues while
-- the other weapon is in hand, and a thrown grenade has left regardless of what is being
-- held afterwards.
function Weapon:update(dt)
	if self._cooldown > 0.0 then
		self._cooldown = math.max(0.0, self._cooldown - dt)
	end
end

function Weapon:ready()
	return self._cooldown <= 0.0
end

-- The trigger, one press per call -- Main edge-triggers the button, so nothing here has to
-- think about a key being held. Returns true when something actually went off.
function Weapon:attack()
	return false
end

-- The alternate attack (right mouse). Left unanswered here, and unanswered is the normal
-- case: the loadout falls through to the shared rifle butt (Scripts/Weapons/Melee), so the
-- swing still lands with a grenade in hand. Override it only for a weapon that has an
-- alternate of its own -- an underslung launcher, a bayonet -- and return true to claim the
-- button.
function Weapon:altAttack()
	return false
end

-- The reload action (R). Offered to *every* weapon in the loadout rather than only the
-- drawn one, because topping up a half-empty rifle with a grenade in hand is exactly the
-- lull the key exists for. Returns true when a reload began.
function Weapon:beginReload()
	return false
end

-- Ammunition, asked and answered in the weapon's own units. full() is what gates a pickup
-- being taken at all, so a crate is left standing on the ground until it is worth
-- something; supply() adds and returns whether any of it was taken.
function Weapon:full()
	return true
end

function Weapon:supply(amount)
	return false
end

-- Is there an aim worth drawing a reticle for? Asked of the drawn weapon only, so the
-- crosshair follows what is in hand rather than being wired to the rifle's magazine.
function Weapon:wantsCrosshair()
	return true
end

-- Where the view model goes while this weapon is the one in hand, and what becomes of it
-- while it is not. Called from Main:postUpdate after the camera has been placed from the
-- same eye and aim, so a model is welded to the view rather than trailing a frame behind
-- it.
function Weapon:place()
end

function Weapon:stow()
end
]]></text>
</object>
